t horizontal, so it can meet \\(R\\) in at most one point; consequently the only way to cover \\(n-2\\) distinct points of \\(R\\) is to use \\(n-2\\) **vertical** lines \\(x=c\\).\n\nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains only the points of the column \\(\\{(c,b)\\mid b\\ge1\\}\\).  \nThus the two columns whose points of \\(R\\) are *not* covered by a vertical line each contain at least two points of \\(T_n\\) (the point of the bottom row and at least one point above it).  \nA sunny line can intersect a given column in at most one point, so the two sunny lines cannot cover all points of those two columns.  This contradiction shows that\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq 2}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nAssume that exactly one line of the family is non‑sunny; denote it by \\(\\ell\\).  \nUp to symmetry \\(\\ell\\) is either vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is vertical*, say \\(\\ell:\\;x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  \nAll columns \\(a\\neq c\\) must be covered solely by sunny lines.  \nIn column \\(a\\) there are \\(n+1-a\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSince a sunny line is not vertical, it can meet column \\(a\\) in **at most one** point.  \nTherefore at least \\(n+1-a\\) distinct sunny lines are needed for column \\(a\\).  \nSumming over all \\(a\\neq c\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{a\\neq c}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n+1-c)\\ge 2(n-1),\n\\]\n\nbecause each term \\(n+1-a\\) is at least \\(2\\) for \\(a\\le n-1\\).  \nThus at least \\(2(n-1)\\) sunny lines would be required, contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are available.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal* (\\(y=d\\)) the same argument applied to rows yields the same contradiction.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\)*, i.e. \\(\\ell:\\;x+y=d\\), then each line of slope \\(-1\\) meets every diagonal \\(x+y=s\\) in at most one point, and the same counting over the \\(n\\) diagonals leads again to a lower bound exceeding \\(n-1\\).\n\nHence a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line cannot exist, and\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq n-1}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n#### 4.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has integer \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(a\\), so it lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n#### 4.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n\n\\[\ny=x .\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(x\\le n-1\\) are already covered.  The only point of \\(T_n\\) not on a vertical line is \\((n,1)\\), and \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(y=x\\).  Thus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n#### 4.3  \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x - i + 1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(i,1),\\;(i+1,2),\\dots ,\\;(n,\\,n-i+1),\n\\]\n\nall of which satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Hence \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i = T_n\\).  \nEach \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); therefore all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n#### 4.4  \\(3\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=n-t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n-3\\).\n\n*Step 1 (non‑sunny lines).*  \nTake the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=t .\n\\]\n\nThese are non‑sunny and cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).\n\n*Step 2 (remaining points).*  \nAfter removing those columns, the uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge t+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nPut \\(a'=a-t\\); then \\(a',b\\ge1\\) and \\(a'+b\\le n+1-t = k+1\\).  \nThus the uncovered set is exactly a translate of  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Step 3 (sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\)).*  \nApply the construction of §4.3 to the integer \\(k\\): for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = (x-t) - i + 1\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y = x - (i+t) + 1 .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny) and contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(t+i,1),\\;(t+i+1,2),\\dots ,\\;(n,\\,k-i+1),\n\\]\n\nwhich are precisely the points of \\(T_k\\) after the translation.  Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_i\\}\\) together with the \\(t\\) vertical lines cover **all** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe family consists of \\(t\\) non‑sunny and \\(k=n-t\\) sunny lines, i.e. exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final conclusion\n\nLemmas 1 and 2 show that the values \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) can never occur.  \nConstructions in §§4.1–4.4 exhibit, for every other integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the complete set of admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
