f \\(T_{k-1}\\) that were on \\(\\ell\\) and are not lost) and also contains \\((k,1)\\).\n\nNow replace the original line \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\) and add the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell' :\\; \\text{the line through }(1,k)\\text{ and }P .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) does not lie on the line \\(x=1\\), the slope of \\(\\ell'\\) is different from \\(-1\\); it is also different from \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\) because the two points have distinct \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-coordinates.  Hence \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.\n\nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}:=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell^{\\ast},\\ell'\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nEvery point of \\(T_{k-1}\\) is still covered (the only possible loss could be a point that was on \\(\\ell\\) but not on \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\); this cannot happen because \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\) contains the pivot point \\(P\\) and coincides with \\(\\ell\\) in a neighbourhood of \\(P\\)).  \nThe two new points \\((k,1)\\) and \\((1,k)\\) are covered by \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\) and \\(\\ell'\\), respectively.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) covers all of \\(T_k\\).\n\nThis completes the inductive step.\n\nBy induction, for every integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) there exists a family of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).  ∎\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Completion of the original problem  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All of them are non‑sunny and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on the line whose equation is \\(x\\) equal to its first coordinate.\n\n* **\\(1\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n  Put \\(t=n-k\\).  \n  Use the \\(t\\) vertical lines (2.1).  \n  By (2.2) the still uncovered points form a copy of \\(T_k\\); by the result of Section 3 we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering this copy.  \n  Altogether we have \\(t\\) non‑sunny and \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\nThus for **every** integer