            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\nWe are asked to determine all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Reduction to a triangular sub‑problem  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nt:=n-k\\qquad (0\\le t\\le n)\n\\]\n\nbe the number of non‑sunny lines that we shall use.  \nTake the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).\n\nThe points that are still uncovered are  \n\n\\[\nT_n'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge t+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-t\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nT_n'=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le n+1-t\\}=T_{k},\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(n+1-t=k+1\\).  \nConsequently the original problem is equivalent to the following one.\n\n> **Problem \\((\\star)\\).**  \n> For every integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) construct \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(T_k\\).\n\nIf we can solve \\((\\star)\\) for every \\(k\\), then for a given \\(k\\) we take the \\(t=n-k\\) vertical lines (2.1) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by a solution of \\((\\star)\\); together they give a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny members.  \nThe case \\(k=0\\) is obtained by taking the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).\n\nThus it remains to solve \\((\\star)\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Solving \\((\\star)\\) by induction  \n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1  Base case \\(k=1\\)\n\n\\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  \nThe line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and contains the only point. Hence the statement holds for \\(k=1\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(k\\ge 2\\) there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k-1}=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(k-1\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_{k-1}\\).\n\nThe set \\(T_k\\) can be written as  \n\n\\[\nT_k = T_{k-1}\\;\\cup\\;\\{(k,1),(1,k)\\}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe two new points cannot lie on the same sunny line, because the line through them has slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden.  \nConsequently we have to add **one** new sunny line that contains \\((1,k)\\) and modify **one** of the old lines so that it now contains \\((k,1)\\).\n\nBecause the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) are distinct, at most one of them meets the vertical line \\(x=k\\).  \nChoose a line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) that does **not** intersect the column \\(x=k\\); such a line exists.\n\nPick any point \\(P\\) of \\(\\ell\\) (there is at least one because \\(\\ell\\) covers a point of \\(T_{k-1}\\)).  \nRotate \\(\\ell\\) around \\(P\\) until the rotated line passes through \\((k,1)\\).  \nDuring this rotation the slope varies continuously; the three forbidden slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) are isolated values, therefore we can perform the rotation so that the final slope is still different from them.  \nDenote the rotated line by \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\).  \nBy construction \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\) is sunny, contains the pivot point \\(P\\) (hence still covers all points of \\(T_{k-1}\\) that were on \\(\\ell\\) and are not lost) and also contains \\((k,1)\\).\n\nNow replace the original line \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\) and add the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell' :\\; \\text{the line through }(1,k)\\text{ and }P .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) does not lie on the line \\(x=1\\), the slope of \\(\\ell'\\) is different from \\(-1\\); it is also different from \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\) because the two points have distinct \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-coordinates.  Hence \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.\n\nThe new family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}:=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\setminus\\{\\ell\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell^{\\ast},\\ell'\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nEvery point of \\(T_{k-1}\\) is still covered (the only possible loss could be a point that was on \\(\\ell\\) but not on \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\); this cannot happen because \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\) contains the pivot point \\(P\\) and coincides with \\(\\ell\\) in a neighbourhood of \\(P\\)).  \nThe two new points \\((k,1)\\) and \\((1,k)\\) are covered by \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\) and \\(\\ell'\\), respectively.  \nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) covers all of \\(T_k\\).\n\nThis completes the inductive step.\n\nBy induction, for every integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) there exists a family of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).  ∎\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Completion of the original problem  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All of them are non‑sunny and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on the line whose equation is \\(x\\) equal to its first coordinate.\n\n* **\\(1\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n  Put \\(t=n-k\\).  \n  Use the \\(t\\) vertical lines (2.1).  \n  By (2.2) the still uncovered points form a copy of \\(T_k\\); by the result of Section 3 we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering this copy.  \n  Altogether we have \\(t\\) non‑sunny and \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion**  \n\nAll non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) are attainable as the number of sunny lines.  \nHence the answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
