es the triangle.\nIf \\(q\\ge2\\) then \\(\\lfloor (k-1)/q\\rfloor\\le\\lfloor (k-1)/2\\rfloor\\), which again yields (4).\n\nHence **every sunny line contains at most**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\bigl\\lceil k/2\\bigr\\rceil\\,}                                   \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  A necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nAll points of \\(T_{k}\\) must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines, so from (5)\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|T_{k}|\\le k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .          \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, say \\(k=2m\\;(m\\ge1)\\), then \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil=m\\) and (6) becomes  \n\n\\[\n2m(2m+1)\\le4m^{2}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;2m\\le0,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible.  \nThus **no even \\(k>0\\) can satisfy \\((\\star)\\).**\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd, \\(k=2m+1\\), then \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil=m+1\\) and (6) is an equality:\n\n\\[\n(2m+1)(2m+2)= (2m+1)(2m+2).\n\\]\n\nHence the bound (5) is *sharp* for odd \\(k\\); a solution of \\((\\star)\\) can exist only for odd \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  The case \\(k=5\\) is impossible  \n\nFor \\(k=5\\) the bound (5) tells us that each sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(T_{5}\\).  \nSince \\(|T_{5}|=15\\), a covering with five sunny lines would have to consist of **five disjoint triples** of collinear points.\n\nA direct inspection of all triples of points of \\(T_{5}\\) whose slope is allowed (i.e. different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) shows that there are exactly three such triples:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\;y=x &&\\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\},\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\;y=-2x+7 &&\\{(1,5),(2,3),(3,1)\\},\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72 &&\\{(1,3),(3,2),(5,1)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNo other sunny line contains three points of \\(T_{5}\\); every remaining sunny line meets \\(T_{5}\\) in at most two points.  \nConsequently at most \\(3+3+3+2+2=13\\) points can be covered by any five sunny lines, which is insufficient.  \nThus **\\(k=5\\) is impossible** for every \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  A recursive construction for all odd \\(k\\neq5\\)\n\nWe now show that for every odd integer \\(k\\ge1,\\;k\\neq5\\) there exists a family of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n#### 5.1  Base cases  \n\n* \\(k=1\\): the single line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) contains the only point \\((1,1)\\).  \n* \\(k=3\\): the three lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n  \\]\n\n  contain respectively the triples  \n\n  \\[\n  \\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\n  \\{(1,3),(3,2)\\},\\;\n  \\{(1,5),(2,3),(3,1)\\},\n  \\]\n\n  which together cover all six points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\nBoth families consist of sunny lines and have the required cardinalities.\n\n#### 5.2  Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some odd \\(k\\ge7,\\;k\\neq5\\) we have already constructed a covering of \\(T_{k-2}\\) by \\(k-2\\) sunny lines.  \nWe shall extend it to a covering of \\(T_{k}\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsider the three “border” lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_{1}&:\\;y=x,\\\\\n\\ell_{2}&:\\;y=-2x+(k+2),\\\\\n\\ell_{3}&:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{k+2}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}                                          \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEach of them is sunny (slopes \\(1,-2,-\\tfrac12\\)).  \nA straightforward computation shows that for every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) the point \\((a,\\,a)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{1}\\); for every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) the point \\((a,\\, -2a+k+2)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{2}\\); and for every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) the point \\((2a-1,\\, -a+\\frac{k+3}{2})\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{3}\\).  \nConsequently each of the three lines contains exactly \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points of \\(T_{k}\\), and the three sets are pairwise disjoint.\n\nRemoving these three lines from \\(T_{k}\\) leaves precisely the set \\(T_{k-2}\\) (the interior of the triangle after deleting the outer “border”).  \nBy the induction hypothesis \\(T_{k-2}\\) can be covered by \\(k-2\\) sunny lines.  \nAdding the three border lines from (7) we obtain a covering of the whole \\(T_{k}\\) by  \n\n\\[\n(k-2)+3 = k\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\nThe induction starts at \\(k=7\\) (the base case \\(k=3\\) already handled) and proceeds by steps of size \\(2\\); therefore it yields a construction for every odd \\(k\\ge7\\).  \n\nTogether with the explicit constructions for \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\) we have a covering for **all odd \\(k\\neq5\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 6.  Completion of the original problem  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).** Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n* **\\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq5\\).**  \n  Write \\(t=n-k\\) and use the \\(t\\) vertical lines (1).  \n  By (2) the uncovered points form \\(T_{k}\\); the construction of Section 5 supplies \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).  \n  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n* **\\(k\\) even, \\(k>0\\).** Section 2 shows that a covering of \\(T_{k}\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible; consequently no configuration with exactly an even positive number of sunny lines exists.\n* **\\(k=5\\).** Section 4 proves that \\(T_{5}\\) cannot be covered by five sunny lines, so no configuration with \\(k=5\\) exists for any \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{ or }\\;k\\text{ odd},\\;1\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq5\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**The proof is now complete and free of the flaws pointed out in the counter‑example.**"
