ether cover all \\(|T_k|=k(k+1)/2\\) points, we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, say \\(k=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\), then \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil=m\\) and (6) becomes  \n\n\\[\n2m(2m+1)\\le 4m^{2},\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(2m\\le0\\), a contradiction.  \nHence **no solution of \\((\\star)\\) exists for even \\(k>0\\)**.\n\nFor odd \\(k=2m+1\\) we have \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil=m+1\\) and (6) reads  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1)(2m+2)\\le (2m+1)(2m+2),\n\\]\n\nwhich is an equality; therefore the bound (5) is sharp and odd values of \\(k\\) are not ruled out.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  A construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).  \nDefine, for each integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le 2m\\),\n\n\\[\nL_r:\\; y = r\\,x + 1 .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*The line \\(L_r\\) is sunny.*  \nIts slope \\(r\\) is an integer; it is never \\(0\\) (because \\(r\\ge0\\) and \\(r=0\\) would give a horizontal line, which is forbidden – we therefore start the index at \\(r=1\\); the case \\(r=0\\) is omitted), never \\(\\infty\\) and never \\(-1\\).  \nAll the lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{2m}\\) are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\nWe claim that the family \\(\\mathcal L:=\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{2m}\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_{2m+1}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 3.1  \nFor every \\((a,b)\\in T_{2m+1}\\) there exists a unique integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le 2m\\) such that \\(b=r a+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(a\\ge1\\) we can write  \n\n\\[\nr:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{b-1}{a}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe inequality \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-1}{a}\\le\\frac{2m+1}{1}=2m+1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(0\\le r\\le 2m\\).  \nMoreover  \n\n\\[\nr\\le\\frac{b-1}{a}<r+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nra+1\\le b<(r+1)a+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince both \\(b\\) and \\(ra+1\\) are integers, the left inequality forces \\(b=ra+1\\). ∎\n\n---\n\nBy Lem