nIf \\(q\\ge2\\) then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{q}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), whence  \n\n\\[\nN(\\ell)\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(q=1\\) then (3) forces \\(|p|\\ge2\\); moving one step in the \\(x\\)-direction changes the sum \\(x+y\\) by at least \\(|p|+1\\ge3\\). Starting from a point of the triangle, after at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) such steps the sum exceeds \\(k+1\\) and the point leaves the triangle. Hence the same bound (5) holds for \\(q=1\\) as well.  \n\nThus for **every** sunny line \\(\\ell\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,N(\\ell)\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\,}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines must together cover all  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\]\n\npoints, therefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k=2m\\) is even (\\(m\\ge1\\)), then \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil=m\\) and (6) becomes  \n\n\\[\n2m(2m+1)\\le4m^{2},\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(2m\\le0\\), a contradiction.  \nHence **no solution of \\((\\star)\\) exists for even \\(k>0\\)**.\n\nWhen \\(k=2m+1\\) is odd, \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil=m+1\\) and (6) turns into an equality\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2=(2m+1)(m+1),\n\\]\n\nso the bound (5) is sharp and odd values of \\(k\\) are not ruled out.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=2m+1\\qquad(m\\ge0).\n\\]\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nr\\in\\{-m,-m+1,\\dots ,m\\}\n\\]\n\ndefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_r:\\; y = r\\,x + (m+1-r). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Verification that the lines are sunny.*  \nThe slope of \\(L_r\\) is the integer \\(r\\).  \nBecause \\(-m\\le r\\le m\\) we have \\(r\\neq0\\) (the case \\(r=0\\) would give a horizontal line, but \\(r=0\\) occurs only when \\(m=0\\); in that trivial case \\(k=1\\) and the single line \\(y=x\\) is sunny).  \nMoreover \\(r\\neq -1\\) because \\(-1\\) lies outside the interval \\([-m,m]