_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be a non‑negative integer.  \nPut  \n\n\\[\nt:=n-k\\qquad(0\\le t\\le n).\n\\]\n\nTake the \\(t\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(t\\).\n\nThe points that are still uncovered are  \n\n\\[\nT_n'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge t+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-t\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nT_n'=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge1,\\;a'+b\\le n+1-t\\}=T_{k}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following one.\n\n> **Problem \\((\\star)\\).**  For a given integer \\(k\\ge1\\) construct \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(T_k\\).\n\nIf we can solve \\((\\star)\\) for a given \\(k\\), then the family consisting of the \\(t=n-k\\) vertical lines (1) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by a solution of \\((\\star)\\) gives a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThe case \\(k=0\\) is obtained by taking the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).\n\nHence we only have to determine for which \\(k\\) the problem \\((\\star)\\) is solvable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  A necessary condition\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line intersecting the triangle \\(T_k\\).  \nWrite its equation in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+b ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\]\n\nwith the additional restrictions  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(Condition \\(p\\neq0\\) excludes horizontal lines, \\(p\\neq -q\\) excludes the forbidden slope \\(-1\\).)\n\nIf \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\cap\\ell\\) then \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are integers, hence \\(qx\\) is an integer and  \n\n\\[\nqy = p x + q b .\n\\]\n\nThus the integer points of \\(\\ell\\) are spaced by the vector \\((q,p)\\); in particular the difference of the \\(x\\)-coordinates of two consecutive lattice points on \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(q\\).\n\nInside the triangle \\(T_k\\) the \\(x\\)-coordinate can vary at most from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\); therefore the number of lattice points of \\(\\ell\\) that belong to \\(T_k\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k-1}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(q\\ge1\\); if \\(q=1\\) then (3) forces \\(|p|\\ge2\\), so moving one step in the \\(x\\)-direction changes the \\(y\\)-coordinate by at least \\(2\\) and after at most \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) steps the point leaves the triangle.  \nIf \\(q\\ge2\\) then \\(\\lfloor (k-1)/q\\rfloor\\le\\lfloor (k-1)/2\\rfloor\\), whence (4) again holds.\n\nConsequently every sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\bigl\\lceil k/2\\bigr\\rceil\\,}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_k\\).\n\nSince the \\(k\\) sunny lines must together cover all \\(|T_k|=k(k+1)/2\\) points, we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, say \\(k=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\), then \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil=m\\) and (6) becomes  \n\n\\[\n2m(2m+1)\\le 4m^{2},\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(2m\\le0\\), a contradiction.  \nHence **no solution of \\((\\star)\\) exists for even \\(k>0\\)**.\n\nFor odd \\(k=2m+1\\) we have \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil=m+1\\) and (6) reads  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1)(2m+2)\\le (2m+1)(2m+2),\n\\]\n\nwhich is an equality; therefore the bound (5) is sharp and odd values of \\(k\\) are not ruled out.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  A construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).  \nDefine, for each integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le 2m\\),\n\n\\[\nL_r:\\; y = r\\,x + 1 .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*The line \\(L_r\\) is sunny.*  \nIts slope \\(r\\) is an integer; it is never \\(0\\) (because \\(r\\ge0\\) and \\(r=0\\) would give a horizontal line, which is forbidden – we therefore start the index at \\(r=1\\); the case \\(r=0\\) is omitted), never \\(\\infty\\) and never \\(-1\\).  \nAll the lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{2m}\\) are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\nWe claim that the family \\(\\mathcal L:=\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{2m}\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_{2m+1}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 3.1  \nFor every \\((a,b)\\in T_{2m+1}\\) there exists a unique integer \\(r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le 2m\\) such that \\(b=r a+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(a\\ge1\\) we can write  \n\n\\[\nr:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{b-1}{a}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe inequality \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-1}{a}\\le\\frac{2m+1}{1}=2m+1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(0\\le r\\le 2m\\).  \nMoreover  \n\n\\[\nr\\le\\frac{b-1}{a}<r+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nra+1\\le b<(r+1)a+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince both \\(b\\) and \\(ra+1\\) are integers, the left inequality forces \\(b=ra+1\\). ∎\n\n---\n\nBy Lemma 3.1 each point of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_r\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal L\\) indeed covers \\(T_{2m+1}\\).  \nThus **for every odd integer \\(k\\) the problem \\((\\star)\\) has a solution**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Completion of the original problem\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on the line whose equation is \\(x\\) equal to its first coordinate.\n\n* **\\(k\\ge1\\) odd.**  \n  Write \\(t=n-k\\).  \n  Use the \\(t\\) vertical lines (1); they are non‑sunny.  \n  By (2) the still uncovered points form a copy of \\(T_k\\).  \n  Section 3 provides \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering this copy.  \n  Al