{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(|T_k|=k(k+1)/2\\), (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality in (2) holds only when \\(k\\) is odd; for even \\(k>0\\) the inequality is false.  \n   Therefore a covering can exist only for  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ \\text{odd}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sufficiency for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2t+1\\;(t\\ge0)\\).  \n   Define two families of sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_i &: y = x + (2i-t), && i=0,1,\\dots ,t,\\\\[2mm]\n   M_i &: y = -2x + (2t+3+2i), && i=0,1,\\dots ,t-1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *All lines in (4) are sunny*: their slopes are \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\), which are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   The total number of lines is \\((t+1)+t = 2t+1 = k\\).\n\n   **Claim.** Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) lies on exactly one line of the family (4).\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) put \\(d=b-a\\) and \\(s=b+2a\\).\n\n   *Case 1 – \\(d\\equiv t\\pmod 2\\).*  \n   Then \\(d\\) can be written uniquely as \\(d=2i-t\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le t\\).  \n   The condition \\(a+b\\le k+1=2t+2\\) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le a\\le t+1-\\frac{|d|}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely the range of admissible \\(a\\) for the line \\(L_i\\).  \n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_i\\).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(d\\not\\equiv t\\pmod 2\\).*  \n   Then \\(s=b+2a\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   2t+3\\le s\\le 4t+3,\n   \\]\n\n   and \\(s\\) has the same parity as \\(2t+3\\).  \n   Consequently there is a unique \\(i\\in\\{0,\\dots ,t-1\\}\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   s = 2t+3+2i .\n   \\]\n\n   Solving \\(b=-2a+(2t+3+2i)\\) shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(M_i\\).\n\n   The two cases are mutually exclusive, so each point of \\(T_k\\) is covered by exactly one of the lines (4). ∎\n\n   Thus for eve