black** if \\(a+b\\) is even and **white** otherwise.\nLet  \n\n\\[\nB_n=\\#\\{\\text{black points of }S_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(n=2m\\) or \\(n=2m+1\\) one obtains\n\n\\[\nB_n=\n\\begin{cases}\n(m+1)^2 & (n=2m+1\\text{ odd}),\\\\[2mm]\nm(m+1) & (n=2m\\text{ even}).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(B_n\\) is odd exactly when \\(n\\) is odd.\n\nConsider a sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  Write its slope in lowest terms \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\;(q>0)\\).\nTwo consecutive integer points on \\(\\ell\\) differ by \\((\\Delta a,\\Delta b)=(q,p)\\); hence\n\\(a+b\\) changes by \\(p+q\\).  Because \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), we have\n\\(p+q\\neq0\\).  Consequently the parity of the colour of the points on \\(\\ell\\) is\nperiodic with period \\(2\\); in particular the numbers of black and white points on\n\\(\\ell\\) have the **same parity as the total number of points of \\(\\ell\\)**.\n\nLet the sunny lines be \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) and denote by\n\\(b_i\\) the number of black points on \\(\\ell_i\\).  From the observation above\n\n\\[\nb_i\\equiv |\\ell_i\\cap S_n|\\pmod2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSumming (3) over all sunny lines gives\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^k b_i\\equiv\\sum_{i=1}^k|\\ell_i\\cap S_n|\n\\equiv\\#\\{\\text{points of }S_n\\text{ covered by sunny lines}\\}\\pmod2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf a covering uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered, so the\nright‑hand side of (4) equals \\(T_n\\).  Since \\(T_n\\equiv B_n\\pmod2\\) (the white points are\neven in number), (4) yields\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^k b_i\\equiv B_n\\pmod2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBut each \\(b_i\\) has the same parity as \\(|\\ell_i\\cap S_n|\\); therefore the left‑hand side\nof (5) has the same parity as \\(k\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\equiv B_n\\pmod2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2) the right‑hand side of (6) is \\(1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd and \\(0\\) when\n\\(n\\) is even.  Hence **\\(k\\) must be odd (or \\(k=0\\))**.  In particular **no even\n\\(k>0\\) can occur**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Existence for odd \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) and \\(n\\) odd  \n\nFor an odd integer \\(n\\) a *complete mapping* of the cyclic group \\(\\mathbb Z_n\\) exists\n(Hall–Paige theorem).  Let \\(f:\\mathbb Z_n\\to\\mathbb Z_n\\) be a complete mapping; then\nthe map \\(a\\mapsto a+f(a)\\) is a permutation of \\(\\mathbb Z_n\\).\n\nIdentify the points of \\(S_n\\) with the pairs \\((a,b)\\) where \\(a,b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)\nand \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  For each \\(t\\in\\mathbb Z_n\\) consider the set  \n\n\\[\nL_t=\\{\\, (a,\\;f(a)+t)\\mid a=1,\\dots ,n,\\;a+f(a)+t\\le n+1 \\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\mapsto a+f(a)+t\\) is a permutation, the sets \\(L_t\\) are pairwise\ndisjoint, each of them is a matching (no two points share a row or a column), and no\ntwo points of the same \\(L_t\\) lie on a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) (otherwise two points would\nhave the same value of \\(a+f(a)+t\\)).  Hence each \\(L_t\\) lies on a line whose slope is\ndifferent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); i.e. each \\(L_t\\) is a sunny line.  The union of the\n\\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_t\\mid t\\in\\mathbb Z_n\\}\\) is exactly \\(S_n\\).  Thus for every odd\n\\(n\\) there exists a covering with **\\(k=n\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Induction step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge3\\) a covering of \\(S_m\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\nexists, where \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\le m\\).  Consider \\(S_{m+1}\\).  The diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{m+2}:\\;x+y=m+2\n\\]\n\ncontains all points of \\(S_{m+1}\\setminus S_m\\); it is non‑sunny.  Adding this line to\nthe covering of \\(S_m\\) yields a covering of \\(S_{m+1}\\) with the same number \\(k\\) of\nsunny lines.  Hence **every odd \\(k\\le n\\) is attainable for all \\(n\\ge3\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.3  Obtaining the smaller odd values  \n\nStarting from a covering with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k\\) odd) we may replace a sunny\nline by a horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  The horizontal line covers all points of the\nremoved line’s row, so the set of covered points does not shrink.  This operation\ndecreases the number of sunny lines by 2.  Repeating it we obtain coverings with all\nodd numbers \\(k-2,k-4,\\dots ,1\\).  Adding dummy non‑sunny lines (parallel to the\naxes or to \\(x+y=0\\)) we can always reach exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nThus for every odd integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration\nsatisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe parity invariant of Section 3 shows that an even positive number of sunny lines\ncannot occur.  Sections 4.1–4.3 construct configurations for every admissible odd\nnumber of sunny lines and for \\(k=0\\).  Therefore the set of all possible values of\n\\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
