is, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nThe set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Upper bound.**  \n   Obviously at most \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, so \\(k\\le n\\).  \n\n2. **Base configuration (\\(k=0\\)).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n\n   are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and together they contain every point of  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **A sunny line can contain at most \\(\\displaystyle M_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**  \n   *Proof.*  \n   A sunny line meets each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in at most one point, each vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point and each diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) in at most one point (otherwise the line would be parallel to one of the forbidden directions).  \n   Thus a sunny line contains a set of points with pairwise distinct rows, columns and sums.  \n   Order the rows of such a set increasingly, \\(a_1<a_2<\\dots <a_m\\).  \n   Because the sums are distinct and each sum is at least \\(a_i+1\\), we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   a_i+i\\le n+1\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,m).\n   \\]\n\n   Since also \\(a_i\\ge i\\), the inequality forces \\(i\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) for every \\(i\\).  