it. By the induction hypothesis, for each \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) a line parallel to \\(D_c\\) is present among those \\(n-1\\) lines.\n\nConsequently, for every \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) there exists a line parallel to the diagonal \\(D_c\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Determination of sunny lines\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions: the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, and the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThe claim shows that every line in any admissible configuration is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny. Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{\\,x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n+1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, each parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (so none is sunny).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for some integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=c\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal L_n\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) satisfies the covering requirement.\n\nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nThe only non‑negative integer \\(k\\) for which such a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
