**not** parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen for every \\(c\\) the set \\(L\\cap D_c\\) contains at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) in the form \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq -1\\) (the cases of a vertical line\nor a horizontal line are treated analogously).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(b=ma+b_0\\). Eliminating \\(b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na+ma+b_0=c\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (1+m)a=c-b_0 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(1+m\\neq0\\), the equation determines at most one integer value of \\(a\\);\nhence at most one lattice point of \\(D_c\\) lies on \\(L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nWe have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  A line can be sunny at most once, therefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\;(0\\le k\\le n)\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\).  Put  \n\n\\[\nr=n-k .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Non‑sunny diagonal lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le r+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_c\\) are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently\nnone of them is sunny.  The family \\(\\{L_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le r+1\\}\\) consists of\n\\(r=n-k\\) distinct lines and covers the diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{\\,r+1}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The remaining triangle  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nT_k=S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{c=2}^{\\,r+1}D_c\n     =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\ge r+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_k\\) is the right‑angled triangle formed by the diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_{r+2},D_{r+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(c\\) in this range we have \\(|D_c|=c-1=r+k\\ge k\\); in fact\n\\(|D_c|=k\\) because \\(c-1=n-(r+1)+c-(n+1)=k\\).  Hence every di