            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\;2\\le c\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(c\\) we have \\(|D_c|=c-1\\); in particular \\(|D_{n+1}|=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (intersection bound)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is **not** parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) then for every\n\\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the set \\(L\\cap D_c\\) contains at most one point of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq-1\\) (the cases of a vertical line\n\\(x=\\text{const}\\) or a horizontal line \\(y=\\text{const}\\) are analogous and also\nsatisfy \\(m\\neq-1\\)).  A point \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\) satisfies\n\n\\[\na+b=c,\\qquad b=ma+b_0 .\n\\]\n\nEliminating \\(b\\) gives \\((1+m)a=c-b_0\\).  Since \\(1+m\\neq0\\), at most one\ninteger \\(a\\) can satisfy the equation; consequently at most one lattice point of\n\\(D_c\\) lies on \\(L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nWe are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Hence the number \\(k\\) of\nsunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) and put \\(r=n-k\\).\n\n#### 1.  Non‑sunny diagonal lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le r+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_c\\) are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore\nnone of them is sunny.  They are distinct because the constants \\(c\\) are\ndifferent.  The family \\(\\{L_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le r+1\\}\\) consists of \\(r=n-k\\) lines\nand covers the diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{\\,r+1}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.  The remaining diagonals  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nT_k=S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{c=2}^{\\,r+1}D_c\n     =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\ge r+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(T_k\\) is the union of the diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_{r+2},D_{r+3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(c\\in\\{r+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) we have \\(|D_c|=c-1=r+k\\); in particular\n\\(|D_c|=k\\) because \\(c-1=r+k\\) and \\(r=n-k\\).\n\n#### 3.  Sunny lines covering the remaining diagonals  \n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nM_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x+\\Bigl(n+2-\\frac{i}{k+1}\\Bigr). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{k+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), never \\(\\infty\\) and never \\(-1\\);\nhence each \\(M_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because their\nslopes are distinct.\n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\in\\{r+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  Solving the system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na+b=c,\\\\[2pt]\nb=\\dfrac{i}{k+1}a+\\Bigl(n+2-\\dfrac{i}{k+1}\\Bigr)\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\ngives  \n\n\\[\na_i(c)=\\frac{(k+1)(c-n-2)}{k+1+i},\n\\qquad\nb_i(c)=c-a_i(c). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c\\ge r+2=n-k+2\\) we have \\(c-n-2\\ge -k\\); the numerator of (5) is a\nmultiple of \\(k+1\\) and the denominator \\(k+1+i\\) divides it, so both\n\\(a_i(c)\\) and \\(b_i(c)\\) are positive integers.  Moreover the denominators\n\\(k+1+i\\) are distinct for different \\(i\\), therefore the numbers\n\\(a_1(c),\\dots ,a_k(c)\\) are pairwise distinct.  Since \\(|D_c|=k\\), the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a_i(c),b_i(c))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}=D_c .\n\\]\n\nConsequently each diagonal \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\ge r+2\\) is covered exactly once by\nthe family \\(\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\}\\).\n\n#### 4.  Whole covering  \n\nThe \\(r=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (2) cover the diagonals listed in (3); the \\(k\\)\nsunny lines (4) cover the remaining diagonals.  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies\non at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for the extreme case \\(k=n\\)\n\nNow we must produce \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nFor each diagonal \\(D_c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)) let \\(P_c\\) denote the set of its\n\\(c-1\\) lattice points.  Because a line can be drawn through any prescribed\nlattice point with a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), every line is adjacent\nto every point of every diagonal in the following bipartite graph:\n\n* left vertices – the \\(n\\) lines (still unnamed);\n* right vertices – the points of a fixed diagonal \\(D_c\\);\n* an edge connects a line to a point if the line can be chosen to pass through that\n  point and be sunny.\n\nFor a fixed diagonal \\(D_c\\) we need to select \\(c-1\\) distinct lines, each assigned to\na different point of \\(D_c\\).  Hall’s marriage theorem applies because any\nsubset of \\(t\\) points of \\(D_c\\) is adjacent to all \\(n\\) lines, and \\(n\\ge t\\).\nHence an SDR (system of distinct representatives) exists for every diagonal.\n\nProceed inductively on \\(c\\) from \\(2\\) up to \\(n+1\\):\nassume that for the diagonals already processed we have already assigned each\ntheir points to distinct lines, never giving a line two points from the same\ndiagonal.  When we reach a new diagonal \\(D_c\\) we again apply Hall’s theorem\nto the set of lines that have **not** yet received a point from \\(D_c\\); there are\nstill at least \\(n\\) such lines, so an SDR exists.  Assign the points of \\(D_c\\)\naccording to this SDR.\n\nAfter the last diagonal we have attached every point of \\(S_n\\) to a line, and\neach line has received at most one point from each diagonal.  For a given line\nlet the attached points be \\((a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_t,b_t)\\) (with distinct\ndiagonal indices).  Because the points lie on distinct diagonals, the slopes\nbetween any two of them are never \\(-1\\); moreover we can choose the slope of the\nline to be any rational number distinct from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) that makes the line\npass through all its assigned points (the points are collinear by construction).\nThus each line can be realised as a sunny line.\n\nWe have obtained \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering every point of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n*Upper bound.*  From (1) we have \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n*Existence.*  For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) the construction of\nSections 4–5 supplies \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\nFor \\(k=n\\) the matching construction of the previous paragraph provides\n\\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
