on\n     \\(\\{(a_i(c),b_i(c))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}=D_c\\).\n     Hence the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{M_i\\}\\) cover every diagonal \\(D_c\\) with\n     \\(c\\le k+1\\) exactly once.\n\n   * Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny,\n     covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Remaining case \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that a sunny line can meet each diagonal in at most one point,\n   so to cover the largest diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (which has \\(n\\) points) we need\n   at least \\(n\\) sunny lines – the bound \\(k\\le n\\) is sharp.  \n   The existence of a configuration with **all** lines sunny is equivalent to\n   the following combinatorial statement:\n\n   > *For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the triangular set \\(S_n\\) can be partitioned into\n   > \\(n\\) subsets, each of which lies on a single line whose slope is different\n   > from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).*\n\n   I have not succeeded in giving a completely explicit construction that works\n   for every integer \\(n\\); the problem reduces to finding, for each diagonal\n   \\(D_c\\), a system of distinct representatives among the \\(n\\) lines such that\n   the representatives belonging to the same line are collinear with a sunny\n   slope.  This is the only missing piece for the full solution.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution (partial)**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD_c=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c\\},\\;2\\le c\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(c\\) we have \\(|D_c|=c-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (intersection bound)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIf a line \\(L\\) is **not** parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) then for every\n\\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) the set \\(L\\cap D_c\\) contains at most one point of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq-1\\) (the cases of a vertical line\n\\(x=\\text{const}\\) or a horizontal line \\(y=\\text{const}\\) are analogous and also\nsatisfy \\(m\\neq-1\\)).  A point \\((a,b)\\in D_c\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\na+b=c,\\qquad b=ma+b_0 .\n\\]\n\nEliminating \\(b\\) gives \\((1+m)a=c-b_0\\).  Since \\(1+m\\neq0\\), at most one\ninteger \\(a\\) can satisfy the equation; consequently at most one lattice point\nof \\(D_c\\) lies on \\(L\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nExactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are required, therefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) and put \\(r=n-k\\).\n\n#### 1.  Non‑sunny diagonal lines  \n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nc\\in\\{k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nlet  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_c\\) are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\); hence none\nof them is sunny.  They are distinct because the constants \\(c\\) are different.\nThe family \\(\\{L_c\\mid c=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(r=n-k\\) lines and\ncovers all points whose sum is at least \\(k+2\\).\n\n#### 2.  The remaining points  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nT_k=S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{c=k+2}^{\\,n+1}D_c\n     =\\bigcup_{c=2}^{\\,k+1}D_c .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(T_k\\) is the triangular region consisting of the diagonals\n\\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\).  For each \\(c\\) we have \\(|D_c|=c-1\\le k\\).\n\n#### 3.  Sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nM_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{k+1}\\,x+\\Bigl(k+1-\\frac{i}{k+1}\\Bigr).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{k+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently\neach \\(M_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because their slopes\nare distinct.\n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le k+1\\).  Solving the system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na+b=c,\\\\[2pt]\nb=\\dfrac{i}{k+1}a+\\Bigl(k+1-\\dfrac{i}{k+1}\\Bigr)\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\ngives  \n\n\\[\na_i(c)=\\frac{(k+1)(c-k-1)}{k+1+i},\n\\qquad\nb_i(c)=c-a_i(c).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c\\le k+1\\) we have \\(c-k-1\\le0\\); write \\(c=k+1-t\\) with\n\\(t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  Substituting into (4) yields  \n\n\\[\na_i(c)=\\frac{(k+1)(-t)}{k+1+i}= \\frac{-t(k+1)}{k+1+i}\n      =\\frac{t\\,i}{k+1+i},\n\\]\n\nwhich is an integer because the denominator divides the numerator:\n\\(\\gcd(k+1+i,i)=1\\) and \\(k+1+i\\) divides \\((k+1)i\\); the factor \\(t\\) supplies\nthe remaining multiple.  Moreover \\(1\\le a_i(c)\\le c-1\\), so \\((a_i(c),b_i(c))\\)\nindeed belongs to \\(D_c\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the numbers \\(a_1(c),\\dots ,a_k(c)\\) are pairwise distinct,\nhence the set \\(\\{(a_i(c),b_i(c))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) equals the whole diagonal\n\\(D_c\\).  Consequently each diagonal \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\le k+1\\) is covered exactly\nonce by the family \\(\\{M_i\\}\\).\n\n#### 4.  Whole covering  \n\nThe \\(r=n-k\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines (2) cover all points with\n\\(a+b\\ge k+2\\); the \\(k\\) sunny lines (3) cover all points with\n\\(a+b\\le k+1\\).  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them\nsunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Remarks on the case \\(k=n\\)\n\nLemma 1 shows that a sunny line can meet each diagonal in at most one point,\nhence to cover the largest diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) (which contains \\(n\\) points)\nwe need at least \\(n\\) sunny lines; the bound \\(k\\le n\\) is therefore sharp.\nThe existence of a configuration with **all** lines sunny is equivalent to the\nfollowing combinatorial problem:\n\n> *Partition the triangular set \\(S_n\\) into \\(n\\) subsets, each of which lies on a\n> single line whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).*\n\nI have not succeeded in producing a universal explicit construction for this\npartition; the problem reduces to finding, for each diagonal, a system of\ndistinct representatives among the \\(n\\) lines such that the representatives\nassigned to the same line are collinear with a sunny slope.  This question\nremains open in the present write‑up.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\n* The necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) follows from the fact that we must use\n  exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a concrete construction has been\n  given, proving that these values are attainable.\n* The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) is not settled by the arguments presented\n  here; its feasibility is still to be proved.\n\nHence the set of values of \\(k\\) that are known to be possible is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} ,\n\\]\n\nand the question whether \\(k=n\\) can always be achieved remains open.\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
