sfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict.**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, **every** non‑negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can be realised as the number of sunny lines in a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition.\n\n**b. Method sketch.**  \n\n1. **Upper bound.**  Obviously \\(k\\le n\\) because there are only \\(n\\) lines.\n\n2. **A universal non‑sunny covering.**  \n   The \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\]\n\n   are all non‑sunny and together contain every point of  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the case \\(k=0\\) is settled.\n\n3. **Reducing the number of non‑sunny lines.**  \n   For a prescribed \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le n)\\) we keep the \\(n-k\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c\\;(c=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1)                                   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   and we shall construct **exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines** that cover the\n   remaining points  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{k+1} \\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c\\}.            \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(T_k\\) consists of the “lower triangle’’ of size \\(k\\); it\n   contains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) lattice points, arranged on the\n   \\(k\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_c=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=c\\},\\qquad\n   c=2,3,\\dots ,k+1 .                                            \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **A family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).**  \n   Write \\(m=k+1\\).  For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{m}\\,x+\\Bigl(m-\\frac{i}{m}\\Bigr).               \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *The slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{m}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore\n   every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are\n   pairwise different.*\n\n   **Intersection with a diagonal.**  \n   Fix a diagonal \\(\\Delta_c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le m\\) and solve the system  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   a+b=c,\\\\[2pt]\n   b=\\dfrac{i}{m}a+\\Bigl(m-\\dfrac{i}{m}\\Bigr).\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Eliminating \\(b\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\Bigl(1+\\frac{i}{m}\\Bigr)=c-m+\\frac{i}{m},\n   \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n   a=\\frac{(c-m)m+i}{m+i}.                                         \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(c\\le m\\) we can write \\(c=m-t\\) with \\(t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m-2\\}\\).\n   Substituting into (5) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   a=\\frac{-tm+i}{m+i}= \\frac{i-tm}{m+i},\\qquad\n   b=c-a=\\frac{tm+i}{m+i}.                                          \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The denominator \\(m+i\\) divides the numerator:\n   \\[\n   \\gcd(m+i,m)=\\gcd(i,m)=1,\n   \\]\n   because \\(m=k+1\\) and \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) are consecutive integers.\n   Hence \\(m+i\\mid i\\) and \\(m+i\\mid tm\\); consequently both fractions in\n   (6) are integers.  Moreover \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le c-1\\),\n   so \\((a,b)\\in\\Delta_c\\).\n\n   For a fixed diagonal \\(\\Delta_c\\) the numbers \\(a\\) obtained from (6) for\n   \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) are pairwise distinct (the map \\(i\\mapsto a\\) is\n   injective because the denominator \\(m+i\\) is strictly increasing).\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,L_i\\cap\\Delta_c\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\,\\}= \\Delta_c .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently **every point of every diagonal \\(\\Delta_c\\) (and therefore\n   every point of \\(T_k\\)) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).**\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n   The family consisting of the \\(n-k\\) diagonals (1) together with the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines (4) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly\n   \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least\n   one of them.  This construction works for **every** integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only restriction on \\(k\\) is the obvious one \\(k\\le n\\); all\n   integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its direction is different from the three forbidden\ndirections \\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound\n\nSince we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\ncannot exceed \\(n\\):\n\\[\nk\\le n .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).  Hence\n\\(k=0\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le n)\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nSplit \\(S_n\\) into two parts:\n\n* the **upper part**\n  \\[\n  U_k:=\\bigcup_{c=m+1}^{n+1}\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c\\}\n       =\\bigcup_{c=k+2}^{n+1} D_c ;\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n  it is covered by the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(D_c\\) with\n  \\(c=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\).\n\n* the **lower triangle**\n  \\[\n  T_k:=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{m}\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c\\}\n       =\\bigcup_{c=2}^{k+1}\\Delta_c .\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{m}\\,x+\\Bigl(m-\\frac{i}{m}\\Bigr).               \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*The slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{m}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore\nevery \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  Because the slopes are distinct, the lines are\npairwise different.*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Intersection with the diagonals \\(\\Delta_c\\)\n\nFix a diagonal \\(\\Delta_c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le m\\).  \nSolving the system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\na+b=c,\\\\[2pt]\nb=\\dfrac{i}{m}a+\\Bigl(m-\\dfrac{i}{m}\\Bigr)\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\ngives  \n\n\\[\na=\\frac{(c-m)m+i}{m+i},\\qquad\nb=c-a .                                                          \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(c=m-t\\) with \\(t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m-2\\}\\); then (11) becomes  \n\n\\[\na=\\frac{i-tm}{m+i},\\qquad\nb=\\frac{tm+i}{m+i}.                                              \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\gcd(m+i,m)=\\gcd(i,m)=1\\) (the integers \\(i\\) and \\(m=k+1\\) are\nconsecutive), the denominator \\(m+i\\) divides both numerators in (12);\nhence \\(a,b\\) are integers.  Moreover \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and\n\\(1\\le b\\le c-1\\), so \\((a,b)\\in\\Delta_c\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the map \\(i\\mapsto a\\) given by (12) is injective,\nbecause different values of \\(i\\) give different residues modulo the\nstrictly increasing denominator \\(m+i\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,L_i\\cap\\Delta_c\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\,\\}= \\Delta_c .               \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus **every point of every diagonal \\(\\Delta_c\\) (hence of the whole\ntriangle \\(T_k\\)) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Verification of the whole construction\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{D_c\\mid c=k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}}_{\\text{non‑sunny, }n-k\\text{ lines}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{L_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}}_{\\text{sunny, }k