y line can contain at most one of the three points, because a line through \\((1,1)\\) and either \\((1,2)\\) or \\((2,1)\\) would be vertical or horizontal, both non‑sunny.  Consequently at least three sunny lines are needed to cover \\(S_2\\); therefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Reduction to the smaller triangle  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n\\).  \nKeep the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,n-k) .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-k\\) are covered by the lines (2).  \nAfter translating the remaining points by the vector \\((-(n-k),0)\\) we obtain exactly  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the original problem is equivalent to the following one.\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  For a given integer \\(k\\ge 2\\) construct \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of the set \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Open part  \n\nThe cases \\(k=0,1,2\\) have been settled.  \nThe remaining task is to solve Problem \\(*\\) for every integer \\(k\\ge 3\\).  \nA construction that works for all such \\(k\\) has not been completed in the present write‑up; several approaches (fixed slope with varying intercepts, assigning each diagonal to a different line, etc.) have been investigated, but a fully rigorous covering that satisfies the “sunny’’ condition for every \\(k\\ge 3\\) is still missing.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\n* the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable,\n* the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible,\n* the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) holds.\n\nThe existence of configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for all integers \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) remains an open problem in the current exposition. ∎"
