tain\n   a covering of \\(S'_m\\) with the same number \\(k''\\) of sunny lines.\n\n   Finally we add the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny) and, if we need one more sunny\n   line, we also add the diagonal line \\(x+y=m+2\\) (which is non‑sunny) together with a\n   sunny line of slope \\(1\\) passing through the point \\((1,m+1)\\).  In this way we can\n   realise every value of \\(k'\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(m+1\\).\n\n   The construction never creates parallel lines of the same slope, so all lines remain\n   distinct.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   By induction the statement holds for every \\(n\\ge 3\\); consequently for each such\n   \\(n\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nWe work with the set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe six points of \\(S_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y=x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y=2x,\\\\\nL_3 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: (1,1),(2,2),\\\\\nL_2 &: (1,2),(2,4)\\;(4\\notin S_3),\\\\\nL_3 &: (1,3),(3,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTogether they cover all six points, so for \\(n=3\\) every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is attainable\n(the cases \\(k<3\\) are obtained by replacing any of the three sunny lines with a\nnon‑sunny line, e.g. \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\)).  Hence the statement is true for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive construction\n\nAssume that for a fixed integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) the following statement \\(\\mathcal P(m)\\) holds:\