igurations for \\(n=3\\).**  \n   We exhibit four families of three distinct lines covering  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{3}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le4\\}\n   \\]\n\n   and having respectively \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) sunny lines:\n\n   * \\(k=0\\): \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n   * \\(k=1\\): \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;y=x\\) (only \\(y=x\\) is sunny).  \n   * \\(k=2\\): \\(x=1,\\;y=x,\\;y=-2x+5\\) (the last two lines are sunny).  \n   * \\(k=3\\): \\(y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=-2x+5\\) (all three are sunny).\n\n   Hence every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is realizable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n3. **Inductive construction (preserving the number of sunny lines).**  \n   Assume for some \\(m\\ge3\\) we have a family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le k\\le m-1\\) when \\(m\\ge4\\)).  \n   Translate every line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) one unit to the right:\n   \\[\n   (x,y)\\longmapsto (x+1,y).\n   \\]\n   The translated family \\(\\mathcal L'_m\\) covers all points of \\(S_{m+1}\\) **except** those with \\(x=1\\).  \n   Adding the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which is non‑sunny) yields a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m+1}\\) of \\(m+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{m+1}\\) and still containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Distinctness is preserved because a translation does not make two different lines coincide, and the new line \\(x=1\\) is different from all translated ones.\n\n   Starting from the base families for \\(n=3\\) we obtain, for every \\(n\\ge4\\), families with **any prescribed** number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n4. **Putting the two parts together.**  \n   * For \\(n=3\\) the four explicit families give all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  \n   * For \\(n\\ge4\\) the inductive construction shows that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable, while the argument of step 1 proves that \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the theorem.\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
