\nL_1:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIt covers the whole diagonal \\(D\\).  The column \\(C\\) now has the uncovered points\n\\((1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).  The sunny line  \n\n\\[\nM:\\;y=\\frac12x+\\frac32\n\\]\n\npasses through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); together with the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=2,3,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nall points of \\(C\\) are covered.  The family consists of the \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(\\{L_1,M,L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\); exactly one of them (\\(M\\)) is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\;y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\),\nand they cover the six points (each line contains exactly two of them).  Hence \\(k=3\\) is possible\nwhen \\(n=3\\).\n\nTogether with the constructions of §2 we obtain for \\(n=3\\) the three admissible values\n\\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that for some \\(n\\ge4\\) there exists a covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines\ncontaining at least two sunny lines.  By Lemma 1 each sunny line meets the diagonal \\(D\\) in at most one\npoint, so the \\(n\\) lines together contain exactly one point of \\(D\\) on each line.  The argument of\n§1 then forces the existence of a non‑sunny line (the one that contains \\((n,1)\\)).  Consequently **at\nmost one** line can be sunny, contradicting the assumption.  Hence for every \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n\\[\nk\\le1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\n* For \\(n=3\\) the possi