n   lattice points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n   The total number of points that \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   For even \\(k\\ge2\\) we have \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\frac{k}{2}\\),\n   whence the right‑hand side of (2) equals \\(\\frac{k^{2}}2\\), which is **strictly\n   smaller** than \\(|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).  \n   Hence no family of \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover \\(T_k\\) when \\(k\\) is even\n   (\\(k\\ge2\\)).  \n\n3. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge0\\).  \n   *Lemma 2 (inductive construction).* There exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_{2m+1}\\) of\n   \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines that covers every point of \\(T_{2m+1}\\), each line\n   containing exactly \\(m+1=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points.\n\n   *Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*  \n\n   *Base \\(m=0\\) (\\(k=1\\)).*  \\(T_{1}=\\{(1,1)\\}\\); any sunny line through this point\n   works.\n\n   *Induction step.* Assume \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\) exists for \\(T_{2m-1}\\).\n   The “outer layer’’ of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) consists of the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   A_i&=(i,1), &&i=1,\\dots ,2m+1,\\\\\n   B_j&=(1,j), &&j=2,\\dots ,2m+1,\\\\\n   C_r&=(r,2m+2-r), &&r=2,\\dots ,2m .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The sets \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) each contain \\(2m+1\\) points, \\(C\\) contains \\(2m-1\\)\n   points; altogether they give \\(2(m+1)\\) points, exactly the number needed to\n   add two new sunny lines (each may contain at most \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}=m+1\\) points\n   by Lemma 1).\n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}:\\; y=-2x+2m+3,\\qquad   \n   L_{2}:\\; y=\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Both slopes are different from \\(0,-1\\); thus \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) are sunny.\n   A direct substitution shows:\n\n   * \\(L_{1}\\) contains the points \\((i,1)\\) for even \\(i\\) and the points\n     \\((r,2m+2-r)\\) for odd \\(r\\); altogether \\(m+1\\) points of the outer layer.\n   * \\(L_{2}\\) contains the remaining points of the outer layer, again \\(m+1\\)\n     points.\n\n   None of the outer‑layer points lies on any line of \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\) because\n   every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\) is contained in the smaller triangle\n   \\(T_{2m-1}\\).  Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{2m+1}= \\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\cup\\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   is a family of \\(2m+1\\) sunny lines covering all of \\(T_{2m+1}\\).  ∎\n\n   Lemma 2 therefore supplies a covering for every odd \\(k\\).\n\n4. **Completion of the original problem.**  \n\n   *If \\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are\n   non‑sunny and every required point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) has\n   \\(a\\le n\\), hence lies on one of them.\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is odd and \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n   Use the construction of Lemma 2 to cover the triangle \\(T_k\\) with exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines.  The remaining points have abscissa at most \\(n-k\\); they\n   are covered by the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  Altogether we have\n   \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every\n   lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\ge2\\).*  \n   By Lemma 1 the triangle \\(T_k\\) cannot be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines, so\n   no configuration satisfying the statement exists.\n\n   Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those described in the\n   verdict.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
