rom \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) are sunny.  \nA direct check shows that \\(L_{1}\\) contains all points \\((i,1)\\) with even \\(i\\) and all points \\((r,2m+2-r)\\) with odd \\(r\\); \\(L_{2}\\) contains the complementary points of the outer layer.  \nEach line therefore contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points of the outer layer.  \nSince the outer layer is disjoint from \\(T_{2m-1}\\), the union  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{2m+1}= \\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\cup\\{L_{1},L_{2}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering all of \\(T_{2m+1}\\). ∎\n\n**Completion of the problem.**  \nThe three cases described in the sketch give the full classification of admissible \\(k\\). ∎\n\n---  \n\n**Conclusion**  \nThe original answer is correct; the alleged counter‑example fails because a concrete configuration exists for \\((n,k)=(4,3)\\), and the reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\) is valid. The set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
