\[\nT_k:=\\{(u,v)\\mid u\\ge 1,\\;v\\ge 1,\\;u+v\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nConversely, if we can cover the points of \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines, then adding the vertical lines (7) yields a configuration for the\noriginal problem.  Hence the original problem is equivalent to\n\n> **Problem (P\\(_k\\)).**  Can the set \\(T_k\\) be covered by exactly \\(k\\)\n> distinct sunny lines?\n\nThe set \\(T_k\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) lattice\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Even values of \\(k\\) are impossible\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line; write it in the form \\(y=px+q\\) with\n\\(p\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  For a point \\((u,v)\\in L\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nu+v=(p+1)u+q .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p+1\\neq0\\), the right–hand side is a strictly monotone affine\nfunction of \\(u\\).  Inside the triangle \\(T_k\\) the integer\n\\(u+v\\) can take only the values \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\); therefore at most\none point of each such value can belong to \\(L\\).  Consequently a sunny\nline meets \\(T_k\\) in at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nIf \\(k=2m\\) is even, then the total number of points that can be covered\nby \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=2m\\cdot m = m(2m) < \\frac{k(k+1)}2 = |T_k|,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the requirement that all points of \\(T_k\\) be covered.\nThus **no solution exists for even \\(k\\ge 2\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge 0\\).  We prove by induction on \\(m\\) that\n\\(T_{2m+1}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines,\neach line containing precisely \\(m+1\\) points of the triangle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1. Basis \\(m=0\\) (\\(k=1\\))\n\n\\(T_{1}=\\{