\\le t\\le m+1)\\) and set  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{2t-1}:\\; y=-2x+(2m+3+2t-2)\n            =-2x+(2m+2t+1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=i=2t-1\\) gives \\(y=2m+2-(2t-1)=2m+3-2t\\), i.e. the\n   required outer point.  For \\(s=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) the point  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(i+s,\\;2m+2-i-2s\\bigr)\n   =\\bigl(2t-1+s,\\;2m+3-2t-2s\\bigr)\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   lies on the same line; its coordinates are positive and satisfy\n   \\( (i+s)+(2m+2-i-2s)=2m+2-s\\le 2m+1\\), hence it belongs to\n   \\(T_{2m-1}\\).  The \\(m\\) values \\(s=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) give exactly\n   \\(m\\) distinct interior points, together with the outer point we obtain\n   \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) on \\(L_{2t-1}\\).\n\n   *If \\(i\\) is even*, write \\(i=2t\\;(1\\le t\\le m)\\) and set  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{2t}:\\; y=-\\frac12 x+\\frac{2m+3+2t}{2}\n          =-\\frac12 x+m+\\frac{3}{2}+t .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=2t\\) yields \\(y= -t+m+\\frac{3}{2}+t = m+\\frac32\\);\n   after simplifying we obtain the outer point \\((2t,2m+2-2t)\\).  For\n   \\(s=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) the point  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl(2t-2s,\\;m+\\tfrac32+t+s\\bigr)\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   lies on the same line; its coordinates are positive integers because\n   \\(2t-2s\\) is even and the second coordinate is an integer.\n   Moreover  \n\n   \\[\n   (2t-2s)+(m+\\tfrac32+t+s)=2m+2-s\\le 2m+1,\n   \\]\n\n   so the point belongs to \\(T_{2m-1}\\).  Again we obtain exactly \\(m\\)\n   interior points together with the outer point, i.e. \\(m+1\\) points of\n   \\(T_{2m+1}\\) on \\(L_{2t}\\).\n\n   The \\(2m+1\\) lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{2m+1}\\) are pairwise distinct\n   (their slopes are \\(-2\\) for odd \\(i\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\) for even\n   \\(i\\)).  Each line contains precisely one point of the outer layer\n   (2) and \\(m\\) points of the inner triangle \\(T_{2m-1}\\).  Consequently\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{2m+1}= \\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{2m+1}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   is a family of \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of\n   \\(T_{2m+1}\\).  This completes the induction.\n\n   Hence for every odd \\(k=2m+1\\) the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by exactly\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are\n   non‑sunny and they cover every required point.\n\n   *\\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n   By the construction of step 3 we obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines covering\n   \\(T_k\\).  Adding the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (which are\n   non‑sunny) gives a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\n   which are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on\n   at least one of them.\n\n   *\\(k\\) even, \\(k\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Step 2 shows that \\(T_k\\) cannot be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines; hence\n   no configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n   Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those announced\n   in the statement.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe are given \\(n\\ge 3\\) and asked for which non‑negative integers \\(k\\)\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to the triangle \\(T_k\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\nReplace all non‑sunny lines by the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are distinct, non‑sunny and together they cover every\npoint of \\(S_{n}\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  Consequently\nthe points that are still uncovered are precisely\n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(u,v)\\mid u\\ge 1,\\;v\\ge 1,\\;u+v\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nConversely, if we can cover the points of \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines, then adding the vertical lines (7) yields a configuration for the\noriginal problem.  Hence the original problem is equivalent to\n\n> **Problem (P\\(_k\\)).**  Can the set \\(T_k\\) be covered by exactly \\(k\\)\n> distinct sunny lines?\n\nThe set \\(T_k\\) contains \\(\\displaystyle|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) lattice\npoints.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Even values of \\(k\\) are impossible\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line; write it in the form \\(y=px+q\\) with\n\\(p\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  For a point \\((u,v)\\in L\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nu+v=(p+1)u+q .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p+1\\neq0\\), the right–hand side is a strictly monotone affine\nfunction of \\(u\\).  Inside the triangle \\(T_k\\) the integer\n\\(u+v\\) can take only the values \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\); therefore at most\none point of each such value can belong to \\(L\\).  Consequently a sunny\nline meets \\(T_k\\) in at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nIf \\(k=2m\\) is even, then the total number of points that can be covered\nby \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=2m\\cdot m = m(2m) < \\frac{k(k+1)}2 = |T_k|,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the requirement that all points of \\(T_k\\) be covered.\nThus **no solution exists for even \\(k\\ge 2\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge 0\\).  We prove by induction on \\(m\\) that\n\\(T_{2m+1}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines,\neach line containing precisely \\(m+1\\) points of the triangle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1. Basis \\(m=0\\) (\\(k=1\\))\n\n\\(T_{1}=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  The line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and\ncovers the single point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2. Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge 0\\) we already have a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{2m-1}\n\\]\n\nof \\(2m-1\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_{2m-1}\\);\neach of those lines contains exactly \\(m\\) points of the triangle.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nO:=\\{(i,2m+2-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le 2m+1\\},\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nthe “outer layer’’ of \\(T_{2m+1}\\); \\(|O|=2m+1\\) and every point of\n\\(O\\) satisfies \\(u+v=2m+2\\).\n\n*Observation.*  Two different points of \\(O\\) lie on a line of slope\n\\(-1\\); because a sunny line cannot have slope \\(-1\\), **each sunny line\ncontains at most one point of \\(O\\)**.  Hence any covering of\n\\(T_{2m+1}\\) by sunny lines must contain at least \\(2m+1\\) lines, and a\ncovering with exactly \\(2m+1\\) lines is possible only if every line\ncontains **exactly one** point of \\(O\\) and, in addition, \\(m\\) points of\n\\(T_{2m-1}\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,2m+1\\}\\) we now define a line \\(L_i\\) that\npasses through the outer point \\((i,2m+2-i)\\) and through \\(m\\) points of\n\\(T_{2m-1}\\).  The definition depends on the parity of \\(i\\).\n\n*Odd \\(i\\).*  Write \\(i=2t-1\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m+1\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nL_{2t-1}:\\; y=-2x+(2m+2t+1).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=2t-1\\) gives \\(y=2m+2-(2t-1)=2m+3-2t\\), i.e. the\nrequired outer point.  For \\(s=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) the point  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(2t-1+s,\\;2m+3-2t-2s\\bigr)\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nlies on the same line; its coordinates are positive and satisfy  \n\n\\[\n(2t-1+s)+(2m+3-2t-2s)=2m+2-s\\le 2m+1,\n\\]\n\nso it belongs to \\(T_{2m-1}\\).  The \\(m\\) values of \\(s\\) give exactly\n\\(m\\) distinct interior points, together with the outer point we obtain\n\\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) on \\(L_{2t-1}\\).\n\n*Even \\(i\\).*  Write \\(i=2t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nL_{2t}:\\; y=-\\frac12 x+m+\\frac32+t .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(x=2t\\) we obtain \\(y=m+\\frac32+t-\\!t=m+\\frac32\\), i.e. the outer\npoint \\((2t,2m+2-2t)\\).  For \\(s=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) the point  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(2t-2s,\\;m+\\tfrac32+t+s\\bigr)\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nlies on the same line; its coordinates are integers because the abscissa\nis even.  Moreover  \n\n\\[\n(2t-2s)+(m+\\tfrac32+t+s)=2m+2-s\\le 2m+1,\n\\]\n\nso the point belongs to \\(T_{2m-1}\\).  Again we obtain exactly \\(m\\)\ninterior points together with the outer point, i.e. \\(m+1\\) points of\n\\(T_{2m+1}\\) on \\(L_{2t}\\).\n\nAll the lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct: the odd‑indexed lines have slope\n\\(-2\\) whereas the even‑indexed lines have slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\).  Each\n\\(L_i\\) contains precisely one point of the outer layer (11) and \\(m\\)\npoints of the inner triangle \\(T_{2m-1}\\).\n\nFinally we set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{2m+1}= \\mathcal L_{2m-1}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{2m+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal L_{2m+1}\\) consists of \\(2m+1\\) distinct sunny lines and\ncovers every point of \\(T_{2m+1}\\).  This completes the induction.\n\nThus for every odd integer \\(k=2m+1\\) the set \\(T_k\\) can be covered by\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Completion of the original problem\n\n*If \\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are\nnon‑sunny and they cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd and \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \nCover the triangle \\(T_k\\) with the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by the\nconstruction of Section 3.  Add the vertical lines\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non‑sunny).  In total we have \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\ge 2\\).*  \nSection 2 shows that the points of \\(T_k\\) cannot be covered by \\(k\\)\nsunny lines; consequently no configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Answer\n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThese values are attainable, and no other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can\noccur. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
