X+Y\\le k(k+1)\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   After the translation \\(X'=X-k,\\;Y'=Y-k\\) the set (8) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(X',Y')\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^{2}\\mid X'+Y'\\le k-1\\},\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. a Ferrers (Young) diagram of shape\n   \\((k,k-1,\\dots ,1)\\).\n\n   Covering \\(T_{k}\\) by sunny lines of the families (6) is equivalent,\n   via \\(\\Phi\\), to covering the diagram (9) by axis‑parallel lines.\n   In the diagram the left vertices correspond to the possible values of\n   \\(X'\\) (vertical lines) and the right vertices to the possible values\n   of \\(Y'\\) (horizontal lines).  The bipartite graph whose edges are the\n   cells of the diagram is a Ferrers graph; its maximum matching has size\n   \\(k\\) (the \\(k\\) cells on the outer diagonal \\(X'+Y'=k-1\\)).  By\n   König’s theorem the size of a minimum vertex‑cover equals the size of a\n   maximum matching, hence the diagram admits a vertex‑cover of size\n   exactly \\(k\\).\n\n   One convenient minimum cover is obtained by taking the\n   \\(t+1\\) smallest rows and the \\(t\\) smallest columns; in terms of the\n   original plane this yields the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &p\\,x+q\\,y = p\\,(t+2),\\;p\\,(t+3),\\dots ,p\\,(2t+2) \\quad\n   (t+1\\ \\text{lines}),\\\\[1mm]\n   &q\\,x+p\\,y = q\\,(t+1),\\;q\\,(t+2),\\dots ,q\\,(2t+1) \\quad\n   (t\\ \\text{lines}),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   all of which are distinct and sunny.  By construction every point of\n   \\(T_{k}\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are\n   non‑sunny and they cover every required lattice point.\n\n   *\\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n   Apply the construction of step 3 to the triangle \\(T_{k}\\); this gives\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_{k}\\).  Adding the\n   vertical lines (1) for the remaining \\(