\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The two families of sunny lines are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\mathcal A &: \\;p\\,x+q\\,y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb Z),\\\\[2mm]\n   \\mathcal B &: \\;q\\,x+p\\,y=d\\qquad(d\\in\\mathbb Z).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   (Their slopes are \\(-p/q=-\\dfrac{t+1}{t}\\) and \\(-q/p=-\\dfrac{t}{t+1}\\);\n   both are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), hence sunny.)\n\n   Define the linear map  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Phi:T_{k}\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Z^{2},\\qquad\n   \\Phi(u,v)=\\bigl(pu+qv,\\;qu+pv\\bigr) .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   A short computation shows that  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Phi(T_{k})=\n   \\Bigl\\{(X,Y)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\;\\Big|\\;\n   X\\ge k,\\;Y\\ge k,\\;X+Y\\le k(k+1)\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   After the translation \\(X'=X-k,\\;Y'=Y-k\\) the set (8) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(X',Y')\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^{2}\\mid X'+Y'\\le k-1\\},\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. a Ferrers (Young) diagram of shape\n   \\((k,k-1,\\dots ,1)\\).\n\n   Covering \\(T_{k}\\) by sunny lines of the families (6) is equivalent,\n   via \\(\\Phi\\), to covering the diagram (9) by axis‑parallel lines.\n   In the diagram the left vertices correspond to the possible values of\n   \\(X'\\) (vertical lines) and the right vertices to the possible values\n   of \\(Y'\\) (horizontal lines).  The bipartite graph whose edges are the\n   cells of the diagram is a Ferrers graph; its maximum matching has size\n   \\(k\\) (the \\(k\\) cells on the outer diagonal \\(X'+Y'=k-1\\)).  By\n   König’s theorem the size of a minimum vertex‑cover equals the size of a\n   maximum matching, hence the diagram admits a vertex‑cover of size\n   exactly \\(k\\).\n\n   One convenient minimum cover is obtained by taking the\n   \\(t+1\\) smallest rows and the \\(t\\) smallest columns; in terms of the\n   original plane this yields the \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &p\\,x+q\\,y = p\\,(t+2),\\;p\\,(t+3),\\dots ,p\\,(2t+2) \\quad\n   (t+1\\ \\text{lines}),\\\\[1mm]\n   &q\\,x+p\\,y = q\\,(t+1),\\;q\\,(t+2),\\dots ,q\\,(2t+1) \\quad\n   (t\\ \\text{lines}),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   all of which are distinct and sunny.  By construction every point of\n   \\(T_{k}\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are\n   non‑sunny and they cover every required lattice point.\n\n   *\\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n   Apply the construction of step 3 to the triangle \\(T_{k}\\); this gives\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_{k}\\).  Adding the\n   vertical lines (1) for the remaining \\(n-k\\) columns yields a total of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every\n   point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   *\\(k\\) even, \\(k\\ge2\\).*  \n   Step 2 shows that the outer diagonal of \\(T_{k}\\) cannot be covered by\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines; consequently no configuration with exactly \\(k\\)\n   sunny lines exists.\n\nHence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely those stated above. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Feedback ###\n\n**Justification Gaps (need more detail):**\n\n1. **Location**: \"* “For a point \\((u,v)\\) on the outer diagonal we have \\(u+v=k+1\\); the two possible equations are\"\n   **Issue**: * The argument assumes that every point on the outer diagonal is simultaneously on a line of each of the two families \\((p,q)\\) and \\((q,p)\\). The problem only requires that each point be on some sunny line, not on both. Consequently the deduction that \\(\\gamma,\\delta\\) must be the multiples \\(p+q,2(p+q),\\dots ,k(p+q)\\) and the ensuing conclusion that \\(k\\) must be odd are unfounded. This breaks the logical chain that rules out even \\(k\\).\n\n2. **Location**: \"* “Suppose the \\(k\\) lines belong to two families with normal vectors \\((p,q)\\) and \\((q,p)\\) (otherwise one family would already contain \\(k\\) distinct lines, contradicting the fact that a sunny line can intersect a diagonal only once).”\"\n   **Issue**: * It is not proved that the set of \\(k\\) sunny lines must split into exactly two such families. A configuration could consist of more than two families, or all \\(k\\) lines could belong to a single family, without violating the “one point per diagonal” property. The claim is asserted without justification.\n\n3. **Location**: \"* “A short computation shows that\"\n   **Issue**: * No detailed derivation is provided. The reader must verify that for every \\((u,v)\\) with \\(u+v\\le k+1\\) the image \\((X,Y)=(pu+qv,qu+pv)\\) indeed satisfies the three inequalities, and conversely that every integer pair satisfying them arises from some \\((u,v)\\) in \\(T_k\\). The absence of this verification leaves a gap.\n\n4. **Location**: \"* “Covering \\(T_{k}\\) by sunny lines of the families (6) is equivalent, via \\(\\Phi\\), to covering the diagram (9) by axis‑parallel lines… By König’s theorem the size of a minimum vertex‑cover equals the size of a maximum matching, hence the diagram admits a vertex‑cover of size exactly \\(k\\).”\"\n   **Issue**: * The equivalence between covering points of \\(T_k\\) and covering cells of the Ferrers diagram by vertical/horizontal lines is stated without proof. Moreover, the existence of a vertex‑cover of size \\(k\\) is asserted by invoking König’s theorem, but the argument that the maximum matching indeed has size \\(k\\) (the outer diagonal) is not rigorously established. The construction of the explicit cover (10) is given without demonstrating that every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on at least one of those lines.\n\n5. **Location**: \"* “Apply the construction of step 3 to the triangle \\(T_{k}\\); this gives exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_{k}\\).”\"\n   **Issue**: * The claim relies on the unproved correctness of the construction in Step 3. Since Step 3