                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{k}:=\\{(u,v)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid u+v\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad k\\ge 0 .\n\\]\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) we have to decide for which non‑negative\n\\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at\nleast one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are **sunny** (i.e. not parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to the triangle \\(T_{k}\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nThe points that are still uncovered are exactly the points of the\nright‑angled integer triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_{k}=\\{(u,v)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid u+v\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) lattice points.\nHence the original problem is equivalent to\n\n> **\\((P_{k})\\).**  *Can the set \\(T_{k}\\) be covered by exactly \\(k\\)\n> distinct sunny lines?*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Even values of \\(k\\) are impossible\n\nAssume that \\((P_{k})\\) has a solution with \\(k\\ge2\\) even.\nWrite each sunny line in primitive integer form  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha x+\\beta y=\\gamma ,\\qquad\n\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(\\alpha,\\beta)=1,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nand multiply by \\(-1\\) if necessary so that \\(\\alpha,\\beta>0\\).\nBecause the line is sunny we have \\(\\alpha\\neq\\beta\\); consequently\neither \\(\\alpha>\\beta\\) or \\(\\alpha<\\beta\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Two families of lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:=\\{\\,\\text{lines with }\\alpha>\\beta\\,\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal B:=\\{\\,\\text{lines with }\\alpha<\\beta\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery sunny line belongs to exactly one of the families, so the\n\\(k\\) lines are partitioned into \\(\\mathcal A\\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The outer diagonal forces a partition of the points\n\nThe outer diagonal of \\(T_{k}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nD_{k+1}:=\\{(u,k+1-u)\\mid u=1,2,\\dots ,k\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA line can meet a diagonal in **at most one** point, because solving\n\\(\\alpha x+\\beta y=\\gamma\\) together with \\(x+y=s\\) gives the unique\nsolution  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{\\beta s-\\gamma}{\\alpha+\\beta},\\qquad\ny=s-x .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince there are exactly \\(k\\) points on \\(D_{k+1}\\) and we have \\(k\\)\nlines, each line must meet \\(D_{k+1}\\) in a distinct point.\nConsequently the map  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\longmapsto u_{\\ell}\\qquad\n\\bigl(\\text{where }\\ell\\text{ meets }D_{k+1}\\text{ at }(u_{\\ell},k+1-u_{\\ell})\\bigr)\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nis a bijection from the set of lines onto \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\nFor a line of family \\(\\mathcal A\\) we have \\(\\alpha>\\beta\\); inserting\n\\((u_{\\ell},k+1-u_{\\ell})\\) into (3) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\gamma_{\\ell}= \\alpha u_{\\ell}+\\beta(k+1-u_{\\ell})\n               =(\\alpha-\\beta)u_{\\ell}+\\beta(k+1). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\alpha-\\beta>0\\), the right–hand side of (7) is a strictly\nincreasing function of \\(u_{\\ell}\\).  Hence two different lines of\n\\(\\mathcal A\\) cannot correspond to the same value of \\(u\\); the map\n\\(u\\mapsto\\) “line of \\(\\mathcal A\\) covering \\((u,k+1-u)\\)” is\ninjective.  The same argument with the inequality \\(\\alpha<\\beta\\) shows\nthat the map is also injective on \\(\\mathcal B\\).  Therefore the set\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) is partitioned into  \n\n\\[\nU_{\\mathcal A}:=\\{u_{\\ell}\\mid\\ell\\in\\mathcal A\\},\\qquad\nU_{\\mathcal B}:=\\{u_{\\ell}\\mid\\ell\\in\\mathcal B\\},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(|U_{\\mathcal A}|+|U_{\\mathcal B}|=k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  All lines of a given family have the same coefficients\n\nTake a line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal A\\) and write \\(\\alpha=p,\\;\\beta=q\\) with\n\\(p>q>0\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  Equation (7) becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\gamma_{\\ell}= (p-q)u_{\\ell}+q(k+1). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince the right–hand side is linear in \\(u_{\\ell}\\) with slope\n\\(p-q\\), the set of \\(\\gamma\\)-values contributed by \\(\\mathcal A\\) is an\narithmetic progression with common difference \\(p-q\\).  The same holds\nfor any other line of \\(\\mathcal A\\); consequently **all lines of\n\\(\\mathcal A\\) must have the same ordered pair \\((p,q)\\)**, otherwise the\n\\(\\gamma\\)-values would not be distinct (two different slopes would\nproduce two different arithmetic progressions that inevitably intersect,\ncontradicting the distinctness of the lines).  An identical argument\nshows that all lines of \\(\\mathcal B\\) have a common ordered pair\n\\((q,p)\\) (the roles of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are interchanged because\n\\(\\alpha<\\beta\\) in \\(\\mathcal B\\)).\n\nThus there exist positive coprime integers \\(p>q\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:\\;p x+q y=c,\\qquad\n\\mathcal B:\\;q x+p y=d,\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwhere the constants \\(c\\) (resp. \\(d\\)) are distinct for different lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.4  Determining \\(p+q\\) and \\(|p-q|\\)\n\nLet a line of \\(\\mathcal A\\) cover the point \\((u,k+1-u)\\in D_{k+1}\\).\nFrom (9) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nc=(p-q)u+q(k+1). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, a line of \\(\\mathcal B\\) covering \\((u,k+1-u)\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nd=-(p-q)u+p(k+1). \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAdding (11) and (12) eliminates \\(u\\) and yields  \n\n\\[\nc+d=(p+q)(k+1). \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nBecause the \\(k\\) constants \\(c\\) (resp. \\(d\\)) are all distinct, the set\n\\(\\{c\\mid\\ell\\in\\mathcal A\\}\\cup\\{d\\