                "content": "### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nD_{s}:=\\{(u,v)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid u+v=s\\},\\qquad s\\ge2 .\n\\]\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) we are asked for all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying  \n\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are **sunny** (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a triangle\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nThe points that are still uncovered are precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_{k}:=\\{(u,v)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid u+v\\le k+1\\},\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nthe integer points of a right‑angled triangle of side‑length \\(k\\); it contains  \n\n\\[\n|T_{k}|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\]\n\npoints.  Consequently the original problem is equivalent to\n\n> **\\((P_{k})\\).**  *Can the set \\(T_{k}\\) be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n> lines?*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Even values of \\(k\\) are impossible  \n\nAssume that a covering of \\(T_{k}\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\nWrite each line in primitive integer form  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha x+\\beta y=\\gamma ,\\qquad \\gcd(\\alpha,\\beta)=1,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\neq0\\) and \\(\\alpha\\neq\\beta\\) (the last condition excludes\nparallelism to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nFor a line \\(L\\) denote by \\((\\alpha_{L},\\beta_{L})\\) its normal vector and by\n\\(\\gamma_{L}\\) its constant term.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.1 (all lines have the same unordered pair of coefficients)\n\nLet \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) be two sunny lines covering