-----------\n#### Lemma 2.2 (the constants are the first \\(k\\) multiples of \\(p+q\\))\n\nLet \\((u,v)\\in D_{k+1}\\) be covered by a line of family \\(\\mathcal A\\),\nso \\(p u+q v=c\\).  Its symmetric point \\((v,u)\\) is covered by a line of\nfamily \\(\\mathcal B\\), say \\(q v+p u=d\\).  Adding the two equalities gives  \n\n\\[\n(p+q)(u+v)=c+d. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(u+v=k+1\\), (5) yields  \n\n\\[\nc+d=(p+q)(k+1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus for every unordered pair of symmetric points the sum of the two\ncorresponding constants equals the same number \\((p+q)(k+1)\\).\nAll constants are positive, and the smallest possible constant occurs at the\npoint \\((1,k)\\) (or \\((k,1)\\)), giving \\(p+q\\); the largest occurs at\n\\((k,1)\\) (or \\((1,k)\\)), giving \\(k(p+q)\\).  Because the \\(k\\) constants are\npairwise distinct and each of them is a divisor of \\((p+q)(k+1)\\) by (6), they\nmust be exactly  \n\n\\[\np+q,\\;2(p+q),\\;\\dots ,\\;k(p+q). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIn particular the sum \\(p+q\\) divides each constant, and the largest constant\nis \\(k(p+q)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom (7) we obtain  \n\n\\[\np+q=k. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the constant belonging to the point \\((1,k)\\) equals \\(p+q\\); but this\npoint also satisfies \\(p\\cdot1+q\\cdot k = p+qk = k(p+q)\\) by (7), whence\n\\(p+q=k\\).  Consequently \\(p+q\\) is odd, and therefore **\\(k\\) must be odd**.\nThus no covering exists for even \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\).  Choose  \n\n\\[\np=t+1,\\qquad q=t .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(p+q=k\\) and \\(p>q\\).  The two families (4) become  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:\\;p\\,x+q\\,y=c,\\qquad \n\\mathcal B:\\;q\\,x+p\\,y=d, \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nand both slopes \\(-p/q\\) and \\(-q/p\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence\nall lines in (10) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The linear map \\(\\Phi\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\Phi:T_{k}\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Z^{2},\\qquad\n\\Phi(u,v)=\\bigl(pu+qv,\\;qu+pv\\bigr). \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3.1 (image of \\(\\Phi\\))\n\n\\[\n\\Phi(T_{k})=\n\\Bigl\\{(X,Y)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\;\\Big|\\;\nX\\ge k,\\;Y\\ge k,\\;X+Y\\le k(k+1)\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((u,v)\\in T_{k}\\).  Because \\(u,v\\ge1\\) and \\(p,q\\ge1\\),\n\n\\[\nX=pu+qv\\ge p+q=k,\\qquad Y=qu+pv\\ge k .\n\\]\n\nMoreover  \n\n\\[\nX+Y=(p+q)(u+v)=k(u+v)\\le k(k+1),\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\Phi(u,v)\\) satisfies the three inequalities.\n\nConversely, let \\((X,Y)\\) satisfy (12).  Set  \n\n\\[\nu=\\frac{pX-qY}{p^{2}-q^{2}},\\qquad\nv=\\frac{pY-qX}{p^{2}-q^{2}} .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p^{2}-q^{2}=(p-q)(p+q)=(p-q)k\\) and \\(p-q=1\\), the denominators equal\n\\(k\\).  Using the inequalities \\(X\\ge k,\\;Y\\ge k\\) and \\(X+Y\\le k(k+1)\\) one\nchecks that \\(u,v\\) are positive integers and that \\(u+v\\le k+1\\).  A direct\nsubstitution shows \\(pu+qv=X\\) and \\(qu+pv=Y\\); hence \\((u,v)\\in T_{k}\\) and\n\\(\\Phi(u,v)=(X,Y)\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  From sunny lines to axis‑parallel lines\n\nFor a line of family \\(\\mathcal A\\) we have \\(pu+qv=c\\); by (11) this is\nexactly the condition \\(X=c\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal A\\) corresponds to a **vertical**\nline \\(X=c\\) in the \\((X,Y)\\)–plane.  Similarly, a line of family \\(\\mathcal B\\)\ncorresponds to a **horizontal** line \\(Y=d\\).  Therefore\n\n> covering all points of \\(T_{k}\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines from the families\n> (10) is equivalent, via the bijection \\(\\Phi\\), to covering the set\n> \\(\\Phi(T_{k})\\) by \\(k\\) axis‑parallel lines.\n\nAfter the translation  \n\n\\[\nX'=X-k,\\qquad Y'=Y-k,\n\\]\n\nthe set \\(\\Phi(T_{k})\\) becomes the Ferrers diagram  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{k}:=\\{(X',Y')\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^{2}\\mid X'+Y'\\le k-1\\}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem is now: *cover the diagram \\(\\mathcal D_{k}\\) by \\(k\\) vertical or\nhorizontal lines*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Maximum matching in the diagram\n\nConsider the bipartite graph whose left vertices are the rows\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\), whose right vertices are the columns\n\\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\), and where a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i+j\\le k-1\\) is an\nedge.  The cells on the outer diagonal \\(i+j=k-1\\) form a set of \\(k\\) edges,\nno two sharing a vertex; hence they constitute a matching of size \\(k\\).\nSince any matching can contain at most one edge from each row and each column,\nits size is bounded above by \\(k\\).  Consequently the **maximum matching size is\nexactly \\(k\\)**.\n\nBy König’s theorem, the size of a minimum vertex‑cover equals the size of a\nmaximum matching; therefore \\(\\mathcal D_{k}\\) admits a vertex‑cover of size\n\\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.4  An explicit vertex‑cover and the corresponding sunny lines\n\nTake the rows \\(0,1,\\dots ,t\\) (there are \\(t+1\\) of them) and the columns\n\\(0,1,\\dots ,t-1\\) (there are \\(t\\) of them).  Every cell \\((i,j)\\) with\n\\(i+j\\le k-1\\) lies either in one of these rows or in one of these columns,\nbecause otherwise \\(i\\ge t+1\\) and \\(j\\ge t\\) would give\n\\(i+j\\ge (t+1)+t=2t+1=k\\), contradicting the defining inequality of the\ndiagram.  Hence these \\(t+1+t=k\\) vertices form a vertex‑cover.\n\nTranslating back to the original coordinates, the selected rows correspond to\nthe vertical lines  \n\n\\[\np\\,x+q\\,y = p\\,(t+2),\\;p\\,(t+3),\\dots ,p\\,(2t+2) \\qquad (t+1\\text{ lines}),\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nand the selected columns correspond to the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nq\\,x+p\\,y = q\\,(t+1),\\;q\\,(t+2),\\dots ,q\\,(2t+1) \\qquad (t\\t