\}\\) are equal.\nConsequently there exist coprime positive integers \\(p\\neq q\\) such that\neach line \\(L_i\\) has normal vector either \\((p,q)\\) or \\((q,p)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set \\(\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}\\) is invariant under the map\n\\((x,y)\\mapsto (y,x)\\).  If \\(L_i\\) has normal vector \\((\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)\\),\nthen the line  \n\n\\[\n\\beta_i x+\\alpha_i y=\\beta_i i+\\alpha_i(k+1-i)                \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npasses through the swapped point \\((k+1-i,i)\\).  Since the covering uses\nexactly one line for each point of \\(D_{k+1}\\), the line that contains\n\\((k+1-i,i)\\) must be the line given by (5).  Hence the unordered pair\n\\(\\{\\alpha_{k+1-i},\\beta_{k+1-i}\\}\\) equals \\(\\{\\alpha_i,\\beta_i\\}\\).\nStarting from any index and repeatedly applying the involution\n\\(i\\mapsto k+1-i\\) shows that all unordered pairs are the same.\n∎\n\nWrite this common unordered pair as \\(\\{p,q\\}\\) with\n\\(p,q>0,\\;p\\neq q,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).\nThus each line is of one of the two forms  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{(A)}\\;&p\\,x+q\\,y=c,\\\\[1mm]\n\\text{(B)}\\;&q\\,x+p\\,y=d,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwith integer constants \\(c,d\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nFor every line of type (A) or (B) the corresponding constant is a\nmultiple of \\(p+q\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom (4) and the fact that \\(\\{\\alpha_i,\\beta_i\\}=\\{p,q\\}\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{type (A)}\\;&c=p\\,i+q\\,(k+1-i)\n                =q(k+1)+(p-q)i,\\\\\n\\text{type (B)}\\;&d=q\\,i+p\\,(k+1-i)\n                =p(k+1)-(p-q)i .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(q\\equiv -p\\pmod{p+q}\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nc\\equiv p\\,i-p\\,i\\equiv0\\pmod{p+q},\n\\qquad\nd\\equiv q\\,i-q\\,i\\equiv0\\pmod{p+q}.\n\\]\n\nHence every constant is a multiple of \\(p+q\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince the \\(k\\) constants are distinct and positive, Lemma 2.2 implies\n\n\\[\n\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_{k}\\}\n=\\{(p+q),2(p+q),\\dots ,k(p+q)\\}.                     \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the sum of all constants equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k}c_i=(p+q)\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .                \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, using (7) and separating the lines of type (A) and\ntype (B) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k}c_i\n=(p+q)\\frac{k(k+1)}2+(p-q)\\Bigl(\\sum_{i\\in A}i-\\sum_{i\\in B}i\\Bigr),\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(A\\) (resp. \\(B\\)) is the set of indices of the lines of type (A)\n(resp. (B)).  Comparing (9) and (10) forces  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in A}i=\\sum_{i\\in B}i .                         \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) is partitioned into the two sets \\(A\\) and\n\\(B\\), (11) is possible only when \\(|A|=|B|=k/2\\) and the two sets have\nthe same sum.  This can happen only if the two arithmetic progressions\nin (7) interlace, which forces \\(|p-q|=1\\).  With \\(|p-q|=1\\) we have\n\\(p+q=k\\) (otherwise the smallest constant in (8) would be larger than\nthe smallest value of the expressions in (7)).  Hence\n\n\\[\np+q=k\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad|p-q|=1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are consecutive integers, they have opposite parity,\nso \\(p+q\\) is odd.  Equation (12) therefore implies that **\\(k\\) must be\nodd**.  For even \\(k\\ge2\\) a covering of \\(T_{k}\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2t+1\\;(t\\ge0)\\) and set  \n\n\\[\np:=t+1,\\qquad q:=t .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(p,q>0,\\;p\\neq q,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(|p-q|=1\\); consequently\nthe two families (6) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  A linear bijection\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\Phi:T_{k}\\longrightarrow\\mathbb Z^{2},\\qquad\n\\Phi(u,v)=\\bigl(pu+qv,\\;qu+pv\\bigr).                     \\tag{14