overed by a line of }\\mathcal B\\},\n\\]\n\nthen \\(A\\cup B=\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\), \\(A\\cap B=\\varnothing\\), and the set of all\nconstants occurring in the covering is  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,q(k+1)+i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\,\\}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Substituting \\((u,k+1-u)\\) into the equations of the two families and\nusing \\(p-q=1\\) gives (7).  Because each point of \\(D_{k+1}\\) is covered exactly\nonce, the sets \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) form a partition of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  The\nconstants coming from \\(\\mathcal A\\) are precisely the numbers\n\\(q(k+1)+i\\) with \\(i\\in A\\); those coming from \\(\\mathcal B\\) are the numbers\n\\(q(k+1)+(k+1-i)\\) with \\(i\\in B\\).  Since \\(\\{i\\mid i\\in A\\}\\cup\\{k+1-i\\mid i\\in B\\}\n=\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\), the union of the two families of constants is exactly the set\ndisplayed in (8). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Parity restriction  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 3.**  \nIf a covering of \\(T_k\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, then \\(k\\) is odd.\n\n*Proof.*  Suppose \\(k\\) is even, say \\(k=2t\\).  The partition \\(A\\cup B\\) of\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) given by Lemma 2 contains \\(t\\) elements in each part.\nFor any \\(i\\in A\\) the integer \\(k+1-i\\) belongs to \\(B\\); otherwise the same\nconstant would appear twice in (8).  The pairs \\(\\{i,k+1-i\\}\\) with\n\\(i=1,\\dots ,t\\) therefore consist of one element of \\(A\\) and one element of\n\\(B\\).  Hence the two parts contain a pair of complementary numbers, which forces\na repetition of a constant in (8) – a contradiction.  Thus no covering exists for\neven \\(k\\). ∎\n\nConsequently only odd values of \\(k\\) (besides the trivial case \\(k=0\\)) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction for odd \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\).  Choose  \n\n\\[\np=t+1,\\qquad