by lines from the families (6) is equivalent, via \\(\\Phi\\),\nto covering the set (11) by vertical and horizontal lines.  Translating (11) by\n\\((-k,-k)\\) we obtain the Ferrers diagram  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_k:=\\{(X',Y')\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}^{2}\\mid X'+Y'\\le k-1\\}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\mathcal D_k\\) a vertical line \\(X'=i\\) corresponds to the line\n\\(p x+q y = k+i\\) and a horizontal line \\(Y'=j\\) corresponds to the line\n\\(q x+p y = k+j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 5.**  \nConsider the bipartite graph \\(G_k\\) whose left vertices are the rows\n\\(0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) (vertical lines) and whose right vertices are the columns\n\\(0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) (horizontal lines); an edge \\((i,j)\\) is present exactly when\n\\(i+j\\le k-1\\), i.e. when the cell \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal D_k\\).\n\n* The edges \\((i,k-1-i)\\) for \\(i=0,\\dots ,k-1\\) form a matching of size \\(k\\);\n  therefore the maximum matching of \\(G_k\\) has size at least \\(k\\).\n* Since \\(\\mathcal D_k\\) contains exactly \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) cells, a matching\n  cannot be larger than \\(k\\); hence the maximum matching size is exactly \\(k\\).\n\nBy König’s theorem, the size of a minimum vertex‑cover of a bipartite graph equals\nthe size of a maximum matching.  Consequently \\(G_k\\) possesses a vertex‑cover of\nsize \\(k\\).  One convenient cover is  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,\\text{rows }0,1,\\dots ,t\\}\\cup\\{\\,\\text{columns }0,1,\\dots ,t-1\\},\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nwhich indeed contains \\(t+1+t=2t+1=k\\) vertices and meets every edge of\n\\(G_k\\).\n\nTranslating the vertices of (14) back to the original plane yields the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&p\\,x+q\\,y = p\\,(t+2),\\;p\\,(t+3),\\dots ,p\\,(2t+2) \\quad (t+1\\text{ lines}),\\\\[1mm]\n&q\\,x+p\\,y = q\\,(t+1),\\;q\\,(t+2),\\dots ,q\\,(2t+1) \\quad (t\\text{ lines}),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nwhich are exactly the lines displayed in (