\) together with \\(u+v=s\\) gives a unique\n   solution \\(u=\\dfrac{\\beta s-\\gamma}{\\alpha+\\beta}\\).\n\n   The outer diagonal \\(D_{k+1}\\) of \\(T_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\)\n   points, and each of the \\(k\\) lines can meet this diagonal in at most\n   one point. Consequently every line must meet the outer diagonal, and\n   the \\(k\\) constants \\(\\gamma\\) are all distinct.\n\n   Suppose the \\(k\\) lines belong to two families with normal vectors\n   \\((p,q)\\) and \\((q,p)\\) (otherwise one family would already contain\n   \\(k\\) distinct lines, contradicting the fact that a sunny line can\n   intersect a diagonal only once).  \n   For a point \\((u,v)\\) on the outer diagonal we have\n   \\(u+v=k+1\\); the two possible equations are\n\n   \\[\n   p u+q v=\\gamma ,\\qquad q u+p v=\\delta .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the two equalities gives \\((p+q)(k+1)=\\gamma+\\delta\\).\n   Since the \\(k\\) constants are all distinct, the set\n   \\(\\{\\gamma,\\delta\\}\\) consists of exactly the \\(k\\) integers\n   \\(p+q,\\,2(p+q),\\dots ,k(p+q)\\).  Hence \\(p+q=k\\) and\n   \\(\\gamma,\\delta\\) are multiples of \\(k\\).  Subtracting the two\n   equalities in (4) yields \\((p-q)(u-v)=\\gamma-\\delta\\).\n   Because the right‑hand side is a multiple of \\(k\\) while\n   \\(|u-v|<k\\) on the outer diagonal, we must have \\(|p-q|=1\\).\n   Therefore \\(k=(p+q)\\) is **odd**.  Thus no configuration exists for\n   even \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n3. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2t+1\\;(t\\ge0)\\) and set  \n\n   \\[\n   p=t+1,\\qquad q=t .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The two families of sunny lines are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\mathcal A &: \\;p\\,x+q\\,y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb Z),\\\\[2mm]\n   \\mathcal B &: \\;q\\,x+p\\,y=d\\qquad(d\\in\\mathbb Z).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   (Their slopes are \\(-p/q=-\\dfrac{t+1}{t}\\) and \\(-q/p=-\\dfrac{t}{t+1}\\);\n   both are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), hence 